---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "bum protein powder",
      "cbum protein powder",
      "raw nutrition itholate",
      "cbum itholate review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Isolate Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-isolate/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">CBUM Itholate Protein Review</Fragment>
      Chris Bumstead's Itholate is a 25 g whey isolate dressed up as dessert. The protein is solid. Whether it beats a plain isolate comes down to flavor, sugar and price.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-it">Who Makes CBUM Protein</a>
      <a href="#label">The Itholate Label</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Range, Ranked</a>
      <a href="#range">Itholate vs RAW Isolate vs Clear</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Certification</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#flavors">Flavors and Sweeteners</a>
      <a href="#vs">CBUM vs Other Isolates</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          CBUM Itholate, sold by RAW Nutrition and often searched simply as Bum protein powder, is a real whey isolate with a real 25 g of protein per scoop. The difference from a plain isolate is what comes with it: cereal pieces, cookie bits and candy-store flavors that push calories anywhere from about 100 to about 190 per scoop depending on the tub. If you want your shake to taste like a treat and you read the panel for your flavor, it is a good buy. If you want the leanest gram of protein per dollar, it is not.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict: Solid Isolate, Dessert Pricing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Itholate is built on micro-filtered whey protein isolate, and every flavor we checked lists 25 g of protein per scoop. That is a full serving by any standard, and isolate means low lactose and very little fat. The Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie panel on Vitacost shows a 33 g scoop with about 120 calories, 3 g of carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar and 1 g of fat, which is lean by any measure for a flavored powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complication is flavor-to-flavor variation. RAW builds many Itholate flavors around inclusions such as cinnamon cereal pieces or cookie bits, and those change the numbers. HEB's listings show about 100 calories for Strawberry Bumcake and about 190 for Chocolate Peanut Butter. Same brand, same protein, almost double the calories. If you are on a cut, the flavor you pick matters more than the brand name on the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price sits in the upper-middle tier. A 25-serving tub has listed anywhere from about $50 at some sporting goods retailers to about $75 on RAW's own store and at Vitacost, which works out to roughly $2.00 to $3.00 per 25 g of protein, approximately. That is more than a mainstream isolate on sale and less than the most expensive boutique brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our label-based verdict: Itholate is a good isolate for people who will actually drink it every day because they like the flavors. For tested athletes, the leaner RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is the better buy because retailers list it as Informed Sport tested. We did not taste or lab-test any of these products ourselves; flavor notes below are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Good protein, variable calories, premium-ish price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Itholate gives you 25 g of whey isolate per scoop in flavors buyers rate highly. Read the panel for your exact flavor, because inclusions can push calories from about 100 to about 190.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes CBUM Protein, and What Bum Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            CBUM protein is made by RAW Nutrition, a sports nutrition brand that dates its start to 2019. Its co-founder and chief executive is Dr. Domenic Iacovone, and the company describes its leadership as including Chris Bumstead, the six-time Classic Physique Mr. Olympia champion, and the bodybuilding coach Matt Jansen. An April 2025 Business Wire release described Bumstead as a co-owner of RAW. So this is not a simple endorsement deal: the athlete on the tub has a stake in the business.
          </p>

          <p>
            CBUM is Bumstead's nickname, and the CBUM Series is RAW's line of products developed with him. You will see Itholate sold as CBUM Itholate, BUM Itholate, or on Amazon as RAW Whey Isolate Protein Powder with CBUM Itholate Protein in brackets. They are the same product line. People searching for Bum protein powder are almost always looking for this tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            RAW's protein range is wider than Itholate. On its own store, RAW lists Itholate, a Grass-Fed Whey Isolate, a Clear Whey, a vegan protein, a whey blend and ready-to-drink shakes, with several of those showing as sold out when we checked. For this review we focus on the three powders that are widely in stock: Itholate, the Grass-Fed Whey Isolate and Clear Whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distribution is broad. Beyond Amazon and RAW's own site, Itholate appears at Vitacost, Vitamin Shoppe, HEB, Dick's Sporting Goods, Academy and military exchanges. That matters for price, because sporting goods chains and warehouse clubs sometimes undercut the brand store by a wide margin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Itholate Label: What Comes With the 25 g
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with what is consistent. Across flavors, Itholate's protein comes entirely from whey protein isolate, and RAW lists 25 g per scoop. Isolate is filtered to remove most lactose and fat, which is why the fat figure stays around 1 g on most panels and why lactose-sensitive buyers often tolerate it better than concentrate. If you want the background, our explainer on <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> covers it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the variable part. The Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie panel on Vitacost lists whey isolate, then cinnamon crunch pieces made from sugar, brown sugar, corn syrup, corn grits, rice flour, corn starch, palm oil and cinnamon, followed by natural and artificial flavors, sea salt, a gum blend of guar, acacia and xanthan, sucralose and sunflower lecithin. In other words, you are getting small amounts of actual cereal. That is why the flavor works, and why the calories move.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sodium figure is worth a glance too. Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie lists about 190 mg per scoop, higher than plain isolates, which often sit nearer 100 mg. It is not a concern for most people, but it adds up if you take two or three scoops a day on a sodium-restricted diet. Anyone with blood pressure or kidney concerns should run their protein plan past a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor (retailer panel)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie (Vitacost)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g scoop, 1 g sugar, cereal pieces</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strawberry Bumcake (HEB)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Leanest panel we found</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate Peanut Butter (HEB)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 190</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest calories we found</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One more label note: the Vitacost listing carries a California Proposition 65 warning for lead. Those warnings are common across protein powders, particularly flavors with cocoa or grain ingredients, and they flag exposure above a very conservative California threshold rather than proof of harm. Still, if contaminant testing is a priority, our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to read them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The RAW Protein Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            RAW Nutrition sells three protein powders worth considering: CBUM Itholate, its plainer Grass-Fed Whey Isolate, and a Clear Whey. We rank them by who they suit, then add two labelled alternatives from other brands. Figures are per the label or retailer listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Itholate vs RAW Grass-Fed Isolate vs Clear Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Itholate</strong> is the flavor-first product. It carries the CBUM branding, the dessert flavors and the inclusions, and it uses sucralose plus artificial and natural flavors in the panels we checked. Buy it if taste is the thing that keeps you drinking shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate</strong> is the plainer sibling, and on paper it is the better-specified product. The Vanilla panel on Vitacost lists a 31 g scoop with about 110 calories, 25 g of protein, 2 g of carbohydrate, zero sugar and about 1 g of fat. It is sweetened with stevia-derived Reb A and Reb M rather than sucralose, and the listing says it is Informed Sport tested and Informed Protein verified and made in the USA. Flavors such as Dark Chocolate use a larger scoop and add erythritol, per GNC's listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>RAW Clear Whey</strong> is a different drink entirely: a juice-style isolate mixed in water. Vitamin Shoppe lists about 20 g of protein and about 100 calories per scoop, with whey isolate, natural flavors and sucralose, in flavors such as Blue Raspberry and Fruit Punch. It suits people who find milky shakes heavy in hot weather, but the 20 g scoop means a higher cost per 25 g. Our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">clear protein powder guide</a> compares it with other clear wheys.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing between Itholate and the Grass-Fed Isolate, ask yourself one question: would you drink the plainer one every day? If yes, buy the Grass-Fed Isolate for its cleaner panel and certification. If you know you get bored, Itholate's flavors are a reasonable price to pay for consistency, because the best protein powder is the one you finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Testing and Certification: What We Could Confirm
          </h2>

          <p>
            RAW's marketing says its products carry Informed Sport certification, the LGC program that tests supplements for substances banned in sport. That blanket claim needs narrowing. Informed Sport's own database lists RAW products such as its vegan protein and creatine, and Vitacost's listing for RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate says that product is Informed Sport tested and Informed Protein verified.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Itholate specifically, we found batch listings in the Informed Protein database, a separate LGC program focused on verifying that protein content matches the label. We could not confirm an Informed Sport listing for Itholate itself. Retail listings describe it as third-party tested for potency, purity and quality, but that is not the same as a named banned-substance certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does that mean in practice? If you compete in a drug-tested sport, choose a product you can find in the Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport database for your exact flavor and batch, and keep the tub until the season is over. On current evidence, RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is the RAW product to check first, not Itholate.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, Informed Protein verification is still useful. Protein spiking, where cheap amino acids inflate the protein number on the label, has been a real problem in the industry, and an outside check on protein content is a meaningful consumer protection.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">TESTED ATHLETES</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the database, not the marketing.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Search the Informed Sport database for your exact RAW product, flavor and batch number before you buy. A blanket brand claim is not a certificate for the tub in your hand.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Itholate tubs hold 25 servings of 25 g of protein, which makes the maths unusually tidy: the cost per 25 g is simply the tub price divided by 25. At the roughly $50 we saw at Academy, that is about $2.00 per 25 g; at the roughly $75 list price on RAW's store and Vitacost, about $3.00. Prices move constantly, and RAW and retailers run frequent promotions, so treat both figures as approximate.
          </p>

          <p>
            RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate lists at a similar tub price for 25 servings of 25 g, so it lands in the same range, roughly $3.00 per 25 g at full list and less on sale. Clear Whey costs more per gram: at about $60 for 25 servings of 20 g, it works out to roughly $3.00 per 25 g even at a lower tub price, because each scoop carries less protein.
          </p>

