          <p>
            Compared with the field, Itholate is not cheap but not outlandish. Mainstream isolates on sale often come in below $2 per 25 g, while boutique grass-fed brands can push well past $3. If you find Itholate near the bottom of its range, it is fair value for a flavored isolate. At full list, you are paying for the flavors and the name.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best way to keep costs down is to avoid the single-tub list price. Sporting goods chains, warehouse clubs and RAW's own multi-buy offers have all undercut it. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder guide</a> covers the products that consistently land lower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Flavors, Sweeteners and the Cereal-Piece Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Itholate's appeal is its menu. Flavors seen across retailers include Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie, Cinnamon Crunch, Milk and Cookies, Peanut Butter Cookie, Fudge Brownie, Birthday Cake, Gingerbread, Strawberry Bumcake, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Mint Chip and Frosted Frenchie Cookies, with seasonal and limited flavors rotating in and out. RAW's store shows a 4.9-star average across nearly 2,000 reviews for Itholate, though that is the brand's own site, so read it as enthusiasm rather than independent proof.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sweeteners vary by flavor. Most Itholate panels we saw list sucralose, some add acesulfame potassium, and flavors with inclusions add small amounts of real sugar or corn syrup inside the cereal or cookie pieces. One strawberry listing mentions stevia instead. If you avoid sucralose, check the panel for your exact flavor, or choose the stevia-sweetened Grass-Fed Whey Isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergens differ too. All flavors contain milk, some also list soy, and the facility statements on the panels we read mention shared equipment with other major allergens. At least one flavor, Strawberry Bumcake, lists Red 40 coloring. None of this is unusual for dessert-flavored powders, but it is a reminder that the CBUM name is not shorthand for one ingredient list.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical tip from buyer feedback: inclusion flavors tend to taste best shaken in cold milk or almond milk and drunk quickly, before the cereal pieces soften. Some buyers also report that the 25-serving count feels short when they heap scoops, which is worth remembering when you compare cost per tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. CBUM vs Dymatize, Transparent Labs and Ghost
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Dymatize ISO100</strong> is the default isolate on our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate rankings</a>: about 25 g of protein from hydrolyzed and regular whey isolate, very little fat or carbohydrate, and promotion as Informed Choice certified. It does not have Itholate's inclusions, so its calories are steadier across flavors. If you want one isolate and no surprises, ISO100 is the safer pick; our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Isolate</strong> wins on protein per scoop at about 28 g and on ingredient minimalism, with no artificial sweeteners or colors, and the brand publishes outside lab results. It costs more per tub, but the larger scoop narrows the gap per 25 g. Read our <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs review</a> if a short label matters most.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ghost Whey</strong> is the closest match on personality: licensed cereal and cookie flavors aimed at people who want their shake to taste like a snack. Ghost uses a whey blend rather than pure isolate, so Itholate has the edge for lactose-sensitive buyers. For a direct comparison of flavor-led brands, see our <a href="/ghost-protein-powder-review/">Ghost protein powder review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Itholate stands out is the combination of a pure isolate base with loud flavors. Most dessert-flavored powders are blends; most isolates are plain. If you want both, Itholate is one of the few options, and that is a reasonable reason to choose it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip CBUM Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Drug-tested athletes</strong> should skip Itholate until they can find their flavor and batch in the Informed Sport or NSF database. RAW's Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is the better RAW option for you, and you should still check the batch.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Strict calorie counters</strong> should not buy Itholate on brand alone. The spread from about 100 to about 190 calories per scoop is wide enough to matter on a tight cut. Choose a lean flavor deliberately, or pick a plain isolate such as ISO100 or Isopure; our <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure review</a> covers the leanest option.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sucralose avoiders and minimal-ingredient buyers</strong> will find most Itholate flavors a poor fit. Gums, artificial flavors, colors and cereal inclusions are the opposite of a clean label. The stevia-sweetened RAW Grass-Fed Whey Isolate or Transparent Labs fits better.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Budget-first buyers</strong> can find cheaper grams of whey isolate elsewhere, especially at full list price. If you only care about protein per dollar, buy a large bag of a mainstream isolate on sale and add your own flavor with cocoa, cinnamon or fruit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everyone else, particularly anyone who has abandoned plain protein shakes out of boredom, can buy Itholate with a clear conscience. It is a real 25 g isolate, and adherence is worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Itholate flavor has the same calories</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein is fixed at 25 g, but inclusions change everything else. Panels we found range from about 100 calories for Strawberry Bumcake to about 190 for Chocolate Peanut Butter. On a cut, that gap is a small snack per scoop. Check the panel for the exact flavor before you buy a tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a brand certification claim as product-specific</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                RAW says its products carry Informed Sport certification, but we could only confirm specific listings for some products, not Itholate itself. Tested athletes should search the Informed Sport database for the exact product, flavor and batch, and keep the tub and receipt until the season ends.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full list price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Itholate has sold for around $50 at some sporting goods retailers and around $75 on the brand store. That gap is roughly a dollar per 25 g of protein. Check Amazon, warehouse clubs and multi-buy offers before buying a single tub at list price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Clear Whey to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear Whey has a lower tub price than Itholate, but each scoop carries about 20 g of protein instead of 25 g, so the cost per 25 g is no lower. Buy it for the drink style, not to save money, and compare per gram rather than per tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heaping scoops and blaming the serving count</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some buyers report that tubs run out before 25 servings. Dessert-style powders with inclusions settle and pack differently, and heaped scoops quickly eat into the count. Level each scoop if you want the label figures, and the cost per 25 g, to hold true.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is Bum protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Bum or CBUM protein powder usually means CBUM Itholate, a whey isolate made by RAW Nutrition with bodybuilder Chris Bumstead, whose nickname is CBum. It is sold as CBUM Itholate, BUM Itholate or RAW Whey Isolate Protein Powder, and it lists 25 g of protein per scoop in dessert-style flavors. Bumstead is described as a co-owner of RAW.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in CBUM Itholate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Every Itholate flavor we checked lists 25 g of protein per scoop from whey protein isolate, with 25 servings per tub. Calories vary by flavor because of cereal and cookie inclusions: about 100 for Strawberry Bumcake, about 120 for Vanilla Oatmeal Cookie and about 190 for Chocolate Peanut Butter, per retailer panels at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CBUM Itholate whey isolate or a blend?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is whey protein isolate. Retailer panels list micro-filtered whey isolate as the only protein source, which means it is low in lactose and fat compared with whey concentrate blends. The other ingredients vary by flavor and can include cereal pieces, cookie bits, gums, artificial and natural flavors, and sweeteners such as sucralose.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CBUM Itholate Informed Sport certified?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We could not confirm it. RAW says its products carry Informed Sport certification, and its Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is listed by a retailer as Informed Sport tested, but for Itholate we found only Informed Protein batch listings. Tested athletes should check the Informed Sport database for their exact flavor and batch before relying on it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does CBUM Itholate have sucralose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most flavors do. The panels we checked list sucralose, sometimes with acesulfame potassium, and flavors with inclusions also contain small amounts of sugar or corn syrup inside the cereal or cookie pieces. If you avoid sucralose, RAW's Grass-Fed Whey Isolate is sweetened with stevia-derived Reb A and Reb M instead, per its label.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is CBUM Itholate good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can be, if you choose a lean flavor. At 25 g of protein for roughly 100 to 120 calories in the leaner flavors, it is a high-protein, low-calorie shake. The richer flavors approach 190 calories, which is less helpful on a tight deficit. Protein helps with fullness, but overall calorie intake is what drives weight loss.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where is CBUM Itholate cheapest?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Prices change constantly, but we have seen it from roughly $50 at sporting goods chains to about $75 at the brand store and some online retailers. That is about $2.00 to $3.00 per 25 g of protein, approximately. Check Amazon, warehouse clubs and RAW's multi-buy promotions before paying list price for a single tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
